WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:08.970
Hi! There's a rectangular tank in the garden. Today we'll work out how much water is inside it.

2
00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:18.762
The tank is 30 centimetres long, 20 centimetres wide and 15 centimetres high. We pour in water until it is 10 centimetres deep.

3
00:00:18.912 --> 00:00:30.738
The water is also a cuboid: 30 long, 20 wide, and as high as the water, 10. Its volume is 30 times 20 times 10, which is 6000 cubic centimetres.

4
00:00:30.888 --> 00:00:38.298
Remember: 1 litre is 1000 cubic centimetres, and 1 millilitre is 1 cubic centimetre.

5
00:00:38.448 --> 00:00:44.394
So 6000 cubic centimetres is 6 litres of water.

6
00:00:44.544 --> 00:00:51.690
Now the other way round. We empty the tank and pour in 4.8 litres. How deep is the water?

7
00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:58.530
First change the unit: 4.8 litres is 4800 cubic centimetres.

8
00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:09.378
The base is 30 times 20, which is 600. The depth is 4800 divided by 600, which is 8 centimetres.

9
00:01:09.528 --> 00:01:15.906
One more: when the water is 10 centimetres deep, how many more litres are needed to fill the tank?

10
00:01:16.056 --> 00:01:21.618
The tank is 15 centimetres high, so 5 centimetres are left to fill.

11
00:01:21.768 --> 00:01:29.034
30 times 20 times 5 is 3000 cubic centimetres, which is 3 litres.

12
00:01:29.184 --> 00:01:37.026
Watch out! Some pupils use the tank's height, 15 centimetres, and get 9 litres. That is wrong!

13
00:01:37.176 --> 00:01:43.626
The water is only 10 centimetres deep, so we use 10. The answer is 6 litres.

14
00:01:43.776 --> 00:01:49.506
To find the volume of water, use the depth of the water as the height.

15
00:01:49.656 --> 00:02:04.698
Your turn! A tank is 25 centimetres long and 20 centimetres wide. The water is 8 centimetres deep. How many litres of water are there? Pause and think.

16
00:02:04.848 --> 00:02:13.986
25 times 20 times 8 is 4000 cubic centimetres, which is 4 litres. Did you get it right?

17
00:02:14.136 --> 00:02:28.218
Another one! A tank's base is 40 centimetres by 25 centimetres. We pour in 5 litres of water. How deep is it? Pause and think.

18
00:02:28.368 --> 00:02:40.314
5 litres is 5000 cubic centimetres. The base is 1000 square centimetres, so the water is 5 centimetres deep. Did you get it right?

19
00:02:40.464 --> 00:02:54.522
Last one! A tank's base is 20 cm by 10 cm, and the water is 6 cm deep. How many litres of water are there? Pause and think.

20
00:02:54.672 --> 00:03:07.818
20 times 10 times 6 is 1200 cubic centimetres. 1000 cubic centimetres is 1 litre, so that is 1.2 litres. Did you get it right?

21
00:03:07.968 --> 00:03:19.194
Remember: the volume of water is length times breadth times water depth. 1 litre is 1000 cubic centimetres, and 1 millilitre is 1 cubic centimetre.

22
00:03:19.344 --> 00:03:23.538
Now go and practise in your garden!

